"Explains how and why an intervention is expected to produce a long-term outcome through intermediate conditions and explicit assumptions.\n\nUse when: Design a complex social or organizational change initiative. Test the missing causal links between activities and intended impact.\n\nAvoid when: Only a compact resource-and-output summary is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Outcome nodes form pathways toward a defined long-term goal. Links state causal reasoning and necessary conditions. Interventions, assumptions and indicators remain distinct from outcomes.\n\nBuild: Define the long-term change and relevant context. Work backward to intermediate outcomes and their necessary preconditions. State causal assumptions, choose interventions and identify evidence for testing the pathway.\n\nCheck: Activities are distinct from the changes they are meant to produce. Important assumptions and external conditions are visible. Causal links are hypotheses with an explicit rationale.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing activities directly to impact without explaining intervening change. Treating a pathway illustration as proof of attribution.\n\nExample: A community learning program links accessible sessions to participation, skills application and improved independence, identifying assumptions about transport and ongoing support.\n\nAlternatives: Logic model, Strategy map."
